I find particularly ominous the notion that somebody in power thinks that they
can charge you with smuggling simply for transferring information over telephone
lines that they don't approve of!  I am reminded of Dave Friedman's comment that:

   "It took about 150 years, starting with a Bill of Rights that reserved
   to the states and the people all powers not explicitly delegated to
   the federal government, to produce a Supreme Court willing to rule
   that growing corn to feed to your own hogs is interstate commerce and
   can therefore be regulated by Congress."



>From the Middlesex News, Friday March 5, 1993 - Page 1

Reprinted without permission, for the personal use of the members
of the Extropians mailing list.

Hudson home raided in child porn sweep

By Staff and Wire Reports

    A Hudson home  was among 40 locations nationwide raided Thursday
in the first crackdown on a worldwide computerized child pornography
ring, U.S. Customs officials said.
    The 48 Marlboro St. home of Wayne N. Hart, 39, was the only
Massachusetts site involved in the raids in 15 states, according to
Hudson police.
    Customs officials confiscated a personal computer and up to 100
pieces of child pornography, Jerry Padalino, special agent in charge
of Customs' Boston office, said today.
    Hart said today he was uncertain "what's going on," and did not
want to comment until he was. "I have a couple of inklings, but I'm
not going to open my mouth until I know the whole thing myself," Hart
said.
    The raids may lead to discovery of domestic pornographic
"bulletin boards," said William Rosenblatt, chief of the U.S. Customs
Service in Miami. "It's not only the largest operation in U.S.
history, I want you to know it's the first," he said. "It involves
the high-tech exchange of child pornography - basically what we have
here is a bunch of computer perverts."
    Padalino would notgive the name or address of the individual
involved, but referred to him as a 39-year-old man who lives at home
with his parents and works for a computer company.
    Padalino said the individual was using his personal computer in
link with the computer at his workplace. Hart is a security guard at
Digital Equipment Corp. in Stow.
    Padalino said the suspect used his personal computer to link into
a modem at his place of work to put calls through to a Denmark
bulletin board looking for child pornography. "They're smuggling it
into the U.S. via telephone lines, which is new and interesting,"
Padalino said.
    The phone calls to download the pornography lasted 30 to 60
minutes and were charged to the company. Apparantly the company
computer was used to avoid having the calls billed to the suspect.
"What we're talking about is cost," Padalino said.
    He received information that can generate photographic quality
pictures from a color printer. "They're very good,"  he said about
the quality of reproduction. Padalino said the search yesterday
yielded 80 to 100 pieces of pornography.
    He said it appeared the children portrayed in sexual acts were
underage, but that would be left up to experts to decide. The
information would be turned over to the U.S. attorney's office for
presentation to a grand jury, which could issue indictments.
    The likely charge would be smuggling, he said, but if the
recipient of the material made it available on bulletin boards here
could warrant distribution charges.
    Padalino said the investigation so far found no link between the
suspect and any activity that would bring him into contact with
children. So far there is only one Massachusetts suspect in the case,
he said.
    The locations that were raided nationwide yesterday were
discovered by monitoring who was making phone connections with a
pornography distributer in Denmark.
    Computer transmissions are quickly taking over from traditional
magazine formats among pedophiles, Rosenblatt said.
    No arrests were made immediately, but if possession is verified,
suspects can be charged with federal counts that could put them in
prison for 15 to 20 years, he said.
    The yearlong investigation, named "Operation Longarm," began with
a child porn sting on a Miami man in March 1992.
    That uncovered the existence of a members-only, Denmark-based
computer bulletin boards named Bamse, Screwdriver, and Vestbjerg.
They charge about $80 a year to transmit photos by phone, Rosenblatt
said.
    The quality of the internation computer transmissions by phone line
was so good, recipients could make copies of the pornographic
pictures on color printers in the United States with no loss in
quality, he said.

