> >Not! I think this is Tim May making an error and including a ">" on this
> >line or whatever. Whether I agree/disagree, please don't attribute this

> This points out a problem I've seen several times of people sloppily
> quoting and misattributing quotes.

Actually, in this case the mail software is most likely to be the cause.

The usual mailbox format uses lines such as 'From foobar' to know
where one message ends and another begins.  In order to not get 
confused if a message actually contains such a line, most mailers
will insert a '>' in the beginning of every line starting with "From".

Here's a test:

From

In the above line, I just typed 'From'.  If you see a '>' in the beginning
of the line, that means your mailer uses this quoting mechanism.

Newer mailers have the option of using the Content-length: header
to know where a message ends, but "From" quoting is still usually
done for backwards compatibility.

