Date: Fri, 5 Mar 93 22:28:57 EST
From: eisrael@suneast.East.Sun.COM (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: ECON, GAME THEORY: Re: Justice, Dale Worley, & Libertarian Foundations

>
>   From: eisrael@suneast.east.sun.com (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
>
>   > If you really want unregulated, dog-eat-dog competition in the market,
>   > why not in the supermarket?
>
>   The two cases are distinct because competition in the market as a whole
>   is an instance of non-zero sum interaction, whereas competition for
>   places in lines is zero-sum: if you get the first place in line, I
>   obviously can't also get the first place in line.
>
>That sounds good for a start, but I think that there's much missing
>here.  For instance, any ownership system is zero-sum, in the sense
>that whatever I own, you don't.  The non-zero-sum part comes in when
>one realizes that the possibility of ownership gives one an incentive
>to create ownable things, which is "globally good" in any number of
>senses.  (Apply to intellectual property?)

I'm not sure that's how I'd phrase it. Rather, I'd say that the
possibility of trade turns a zero-sum interaction into a non-zero sum
interaction. I own my car and you can't own it at the same time, for
example. But, the possibility that you might want it more than me and
that you might have something to offer that I want more than the car
opens up a situation that allows us each to be happier with our lot. It
makes a bounded situation essentially unbounded.

In the supermarket example, the people on line could offer something in
exchange for preferred places in line. Perhaps they do in a way:  one
might reap a feeling of "being nice" by waiting one's turn. The
reassurance that one is a good person (according to whatever
definitions one learned in grammar school, no doubt) might be
compensation for not always getting the first place in line. Maybe the
guy who stands behind you with only one or two items and a sorry look
on his face is really offering you a chance to feel good about yourself
in exchange for your place in line.

>On the other hand, there are non-zero-sum games where open competition
>is really bad -- consider the class of "tragedy of the commons
>problems".

Typically, when these sorts of problems arise, it is because the
property rights of the thing held in common have been poorly or
incompletely spelled out. When the property rights are re-discovered or
re-enforced, the tragedy will often disappear. Conversely, if the
property rights are buried, the tragedy reemerges. If my rights to my
car were made blurry or unenforceable, we'd have no way to trade those
rights and the situation collapses back to zero-sum.

>But all of this seems suspect from an anarcho-capitalist point of view
>-- it justifies competition in the name of some sort of global good,
>rather than asserting that competition is good by definition.  Do you
>really want the legitimacy capitalism to depend on your ability to
>*prove* that it gives utilitarian results?

No, I wouldn't care to defend capitalism on that premise, although it
is true that it does produce a greater general welfare. Its best
defense, in my opinion, comes from the fact that capitalism is the
social system most consonant with what we are as individuals. We are
survival machines, built by collections of genes in competition with
one another to out-survive the rest. Pure altruism isn't open to us, at
least not as a stable, repeatable strategy. What advances individuals
the best is what Richard Dawkins calls "reciprocal altruism", but which
is commonly known as trade or quid pro quo. You scratch my back, I'll
scratch yours. And it works precisely because trade allows individuals
to obtain with help what they could not obtain alone by turning
zero-sum situations to non-zero sum. (Interestingly, however, Dawkins
goes to great lengths to disavow his own findings when attempts are
made to link his ideas with the economic realm.  He's a socialist: go
figure!)

Elias Israel
eisrael@east.sun.com

