JAY CONFESSES TO TOXIC THEOLOGY: It comes down to this. I feel as
victimized by corporations as I do by governments. I feel they
both harvest their wealth from the work of others. Sorry about the
toxic theology; (labor theory of value) but in my personal
experience, I've found this to be true: I've worked like hell in
crappy service sector jobs to make money for owners/management,
who pretty much sat around and did nothing.

HOLD THE PRESSES--JAY ONCE EMBRACED THE MARKET: (long personal
anecdote; skip if you want to read the postulates and make me cry)
I was once an Entrepreneur. As a college student, I 'owned' a
housecleaning business with my brother in Syracuse. Don't laugh; I
was able to make twice what officetemps were making in a severely
depressed economy, pushing twenty an hour. The rise of the service
sector! Of course, the work was humiliating, but it was money,
right? Less of my valuable time to the Machine, more time to make
art...

But I decided to pay my brother less than myself--because I was
finding the work, buying the answering machine, doing the
follow-up calls, posting the flyers. Imagine my horror to discover
him, smoking a cigarette on the porch while I was busting my ass
cleaning the other room! The pay difference had alienated him.
Suddenly I was the owner, and here was labor dragging its goddamn
feet. I hated him. I thought of ways to fire him, (my brother!)
and hire other people, and check up on them, to see if they were
working hard enough, meaning as hard as I would. I schemed up
devious tests of honesty, to see if the minimum wage employees I
was planning on hiring away from fast food jobs (attractive
teenage girls, I figured. Why not?) could be trusted in other
people's houses. I would let myself in beforehand, and plant a
twenty dollar bill in a room I'd make her clean during a
'training' session. If she took it, she failed the interview.
Gradually, I build up my stable of employees, codify all this
knowledge into 3 ring binders, someone else is running it, I'm
designing the ads, hiring accountants, talking with lawyers,
insurance salesmen...WHAT? LAWYERS? INSURANCE SALESMEN?
WAITAMINUTE! (vomiting noises)

Ultimately, the whole thing bugged the hell out of me! The game
was fundamentally ugly to me. I graduated and moved, and became an
OfficeTemp, making the same money in a more robust economy, a
haircut, tie and uncomfortable shoes the only sign of my
humiliating submission to the FreeMarket. And I gradually came to
hate it as much as scrubbing toilets! Go figure!

JAYS PRESENT DAY BARGAIN WITH THE DEVIL: I see the Dynamic Dou,
the double-edged sword of industrial culture, the
BenificentState/FreeMarket Delux ComboMeal (TM) as producing
things I have a hard time living without. I also have a hard time
really imagining the one without the other; I think this is the
problem many have with Libertarianism. They can't realistically
reconstruct the model in their minds from the data; and even with
all the Extropians I've read, I have that problem, too. (Snowcrash
does it best, I think, with the all important flaw of providing no
evolutionary transitional forms. How exactly do we move through
memespace to it, one mutation at a time? And Snowcrash is just a
transitional form anyway, so it doesn't really matter...)

There's no utopia in the present scenario, capitalist or
socialist. Just shades of gray, with extremists who get to say
anything they want about the coercionless free market (an oxymoron
