Tim May raises the question of why he does not simply keep a low
profile vis a vis the state. I think one answer is implicit in Robert
Frank's book and my discussion of barroom brawls (Price Theory, 2nd
edn only, Game Theory chapter). There are lots of situations where it
is in your interest to be known to be the sort of person who would do
X in situation Y, even though when situation Y arrives it is in your
interest not to do X. This includes (in some environments) being the
sort of person who beats up people who do not give way to his
desires. It also includes being the sort of person who is honest,
takes the interests of those near him into account, etc. (in other
environments). The first case is the individual equivalent of a
doomsday machine; the second reflects the fact that nice people are
worth more as employees, employers, spice, etc.

If we are not perfect liers (and we are not, for reason I think
explainable), then one strategy for appearing to be such a person is
to be such a person. You might achieve it by altering yourself over a
period of time (synthesizing a conscience, say) or by being
genetically programmed via evolution.

So one explanation of Tim's puzzle is that, in the environment in
which we evolved, being a Milquetoast did not lead to reproductive
success: someone else got the girl, nobody else was willing to risk
his life to preserve that of such a useless member of the tribe, and
when you demanded your share of the limited food during a famine
everyone ignored you. Unfortunately for Tim, or at least for his
genes, he has not yet adapted to a changed environment.

On another subject, also connected to the Waco events ...  . I have
asked several of my colleagues the following legal question: 

Someone breaks down your door, points a gun at you, and yells
"freeze, I am the police." Are you legally obliged to believe him? In
other words, if you respond by shooting him dead, can you
successfully claim self defense?

The answer appears to be that it depends on whether the judge or jury
believes that you reasonably believed he was lying and was a threat
to your life. If the answer is yes, you are free. If the answer is
that you believed it, but the belief was not reasonable under the
circumstances, you are probably guilty of manslaughter. If they think
you are lying, it is murder.

This leads me to a second question. What if your belief is reasonable
in terms of your belief system but not other people's? If a bunch of
armed men break down my door yelling that they are the police, I
assume they are telling the truth, because hardly anyone else acts
like that. But suppose I think I am Jesus and Armageddon is near? It
seems perfectly reasonable to me that the devil may have incited a
bunch of private hoodlums to attack me.

The answer to that is that the court will not take account of my
views in deciding whether my belief was reasonable, although they
might in deciding whether I believe it. In a sense, this means that
the legal system is not religiously neutral--whether I am the second
coming of Christ is a religious issue, after all. On the other hand,
here as in the case of schooling, I think the correct conclusion is
not that they should be religiously neutral (think about the
implications) but that these issues inevitably involve taking
religious stands, and that that is an argument against having them
done by the state.

In all of this I am summarizing my conclusion to talking to several
law professors; I offer no guarantee that I have correctly described
their views, or that those views are correct.

"(It's the drug war and the military industrial complex that have
impoverished this country; not leftist/statist ideology, IMO.)"
(Edward J. OConnell)

On the first, I expect that most of the libertarians on this list
would agree with you; speaking for myself, I have been arguing for
the abolition of drug laws for twenty some years now. On the second,
to the extent that you are willing to accept military expenditure in
the budget as a fair measure of the importance of the military, it
has been trending down for some thirty years. But I think most of the
libertarians on the list would also agree that the government's
military activities have a generally undesirable effect on the
society, although some would argue that that is a cost we must bear
in order to avoid the still more undesirable effects of being ruled
by another and worse government.

Insofar as we would disagree, it is because we have a longer list,
including lots of forms of regulation, welfare, etc.

"But all of this seems suspect from an anarcho-capitalist point of
view-- it justifies competition in the name of some sort of global
good, rather than asserting that competition is good by definition. 
Do you really want the legitimacy capitalism to depend on your
ability to *prove* that it gives utilitarian results?" (Dale Worley)

I find it useful to distinguish between libertarianism and
anarcho-capitalism. An anarcho-capitalist is someone who is in favor
of a particular set of institutions. He might be in favor of them
because he believes they maximize total happiness, or because he
believes they maximize total freedom, or because he believes they
serve his narrowly defined interests, or ...  . A libertarian is
someone who wants a society with a lot of liberty, with "liberty"
defined in a particular way. Whether an anarcho-capitalist society
will be libertarian is a theorem to be proved, not a definition--and
so is whether it will be utilitarian.

One of the thing that bothers me about your quote (and more about
other things I have seen said by less thoughtful people) is that they
seem to treat other people's conclusions as if they were assumptions.
I think very few people really believe that competition (or even
liberty) is good by definition, in the sense of believing that it
would be a good thing whatever its consequences. Most of us tend to
believe that certain things, such as individual liberty, are both
good in themselves and have good consequences, and we support them on
that basis. 

David Friedman

